['Here is the C code that implements the described functionality:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Exactly one argument expected.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    if (strlen(argv[1]) == 0 || strcmp(argv[1], "") != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Argument is not a string.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0; argv[1][i]; i++) {
        while (argv[1][i] == ' ') {
            memmove(&argv[1][i], &argv[1][i + 1], strlen(argv[1]) - i);
            i--;
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; argv[1][i]; i++) {
        if (argv[1][i] == '\n') {
            memmove(&argv[1][i], &argv[1][i + 1], strlen(argv[1]) - i);
            i--;
        }
    }

    argv[1][strlen(argv[1])] = '\0';

    printf("Trimmed string: %s\n", argv[1]);

    return 0;
}
```

This code first checks if the user provided exactly one argument. If not, it prints an error message and returns with a non-zero status code. Then, it checks if the provided argument is a non-empty string. If not, it also prints an error message and returns.

Next, it uses two loops to trim the trailing whitespace from the string. The first loop moves all characters to the left after a space character is found, effectively removing the space. This process continues until no more spaces are found at the end of the string.

The second loop does the same thing but for newline characters.

Finally, it null terminates the trimmed string by assigning '\0' to its last character and prints the trimmed string.']

